1
00:00:00,560 --> 00:00:03,660
Welcome to Chapter 5, Red Blood Cell Disorders.

2
00:00:04,180 --> 00:00:07,380
The vast majority of this chapter actually deals with anemias.

3
00:00:07,840 --> 00:00:11,800
We're going to subdivide the anemias and talk about them in groups.

4
00:00:12,140 --> 00:00:16,880
However, before we actually begin, it's important to gain a general understanding of anemia.

5
00:00:17,500 --> 00:00:22,020
Anemia is defined as a reduction in circulating red blood cell mass.

6
00:00:22,420 --> 00:00:24,580
This is the technical definition of anemia,

7
00:00:24,760 --> 00:00:30,080
and we're well aware of the fact that red blood cells carry the vast majority of oxygen

8
00:00:30,080 --> 00:00:33,620
within the blood, in particular the hemoglobin within the red blood cell,

9
00:00:34,020 --> 00:00:36,500
carries the vast majority of oxygen within the blood.

10
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:40,220
And therefore, if patients have a reduction in red blood cell mass,

11
00:00:40,300 --> 00:00:43,120
they're going to present with signs and symptoms of hypoxia.

12
00:00:43,880 --> 00:00:48,040
Some of those signs would include weakness, fatigue, and dyspnea,

13
00:00:48,340 --> 00:00:52,220
dyspnea being shortness of breath, pale conjunctiva in skin,

14
00:00:52,360 --> 00:00:57,020
and this is a relatively good clinical test.

15
00:00:57,020 --> 00:01:01,180
Pale conjunctiva is a good sign that there is an anemia.

16
00:01:01,860 --> 00:01:05,160
The patient may present with headache and lightheadedness due to hypoxia

17
00:01:05,160 --> 00:01:07,840
to the central nervous system, in particular the brain.

18
00:01:08,380 --> 00:01:11,180
And the patient may even exhibit signs and symptoms of angina.

19
00:01:11,280 --> 00:01:15,560
Angina is chest pain, and because there's low oxygen in the blood,

20
00:01:15,660 --> 00:01:17,540
there would be low oxygen delivery to the heart.

21
00:01:17,880 --> 00:01:23,200
And if the patient has some preexisting situation that has already decreased

22
00:01:23,200 --> 00:01:26,200
the blood flow to the heart, for example, coronary artery disease,

23
00:01:26,540 --> 00:01:31,780
then a further decrease in oxygen in the little bit of blood that's actually going to the heart

24
00:01:31,780 --> 00:01:35,100
could actually result in signs and symptoms of angina.

25
00:01:35,720 --> 00:01:38,460
These are the classic signs and symptoms of hypoxia.

26
00:01:38,940 --> 00:01:42,060
Now, once we have a patient with signs and symptoms of hypoxia,

27
00:01:42,420 --> 00:01:47,660
then the next discussion becomes how we can prove that they actually have an anemia.

28
00:01:47,660 --> 00:01:53,460
And remember that the technical definition of an anemia is a decrease in red blood cell mass.

29
00:01:53,920 --> 00:01:57,320
Unfortunately, it's very difficult to measure the red blood cell mass.

30
00:01:57,440 --> 00:02:02,380
Obviously, we can't take a patient and bleed them and then measure how much their red blood cells weigh.

31
00:02:02,820 --> 00:02:04,400
So it's very difficult to measure.

32
00:02:04,500 --> 00:02:07,200
There are ways to do it, but it would be very expensive and very difficult.

33
00:02:07,820 --> 00:02:12,280
So instead of measuring red blood cell mass, we use some surrogate markers.

34
00:02:12,280 --> 00:02:16,720
And these surrogate markers help us to determine or make an effort to determine

35
00:02:16,720 --> 00:02:19,560
exactly what the patient's red blood cell mass might be.

36
00:02:19,940 --> 00:02:27,000
The three key laboratory studies that are used are hemoglobin, hematocrit, and the red blood cell count.

37
00:02:27,780 --> 00:02:31,420
So these three markers, hemoglobin, hematocrit, and the red blood cell count,

38
00:02:31,720 --> 00:02:33,760
are used as surrogates for red blood cell mass.

39
00:02:34,160 --> 00:02:37,960
Now, you could actually stop me and say, well, you know, why are these surrogates,

40
00:02:38,300 --> 00:02:41,200
why don't we just say that they equal the red blood cell mass?

41
00:02:41,200 --> 00:02:44,860
And the answer is that each of these is concentration dependent.

42
00:02:45,560 --> 00:02:52,680
And so they could, in some circumstances, not be a good estimate of red blood cell mass.

43
00:02:52,960 --> 00:02:57,800
For example, a woman may have normal red blood cell mass when she's pregnant.

44
00:02:58,140 --> 00:03:04,100
However, because her blood volume increases so much, her hemoglobin and hematocrit actually go down.

45
00:03:04,460 --> 00:03:06,860
And because the hemoglobin and hematocrit go down,

46
00:03:07,020 --> 00:03:10,720
that doesn't mean that she's got a decrease in red blood cell mass.

47
00:03:10,720 --> 00:03:15,860
It just simply means that she's got more blood volume, and so she's diluted out her normal red blood cell mass.

48
00:03:16,360 --> 00:03:22,040
So that might be one circumstance in which a patient could have a lower hemoglobin or hematocrit,

49
00:03:22,140 --> 00:03:24,140
but would still have a normal red blood cell mass.

50
00:03:24,520 --> 00:03:30,740
Another example, just to, again, highlight this principle, let's say a patient gets a gunshot wound.

51
00:03:31,640 --> 00:03:36,140
If they get a gunshot wound, they're obviously going to bleed out both red blood cells and fluid.

52
00:03:36,140 --> 00:03:39,460
And so immediately after the gunshot wound, when there's blood loss,

53
00:03:39,740 --> 00:03:41,960
the patient's hemoglobin and hematocrit would be normal.

54
00:03:42,420 --> 00:03:47,360
What's going to show you that there was actually blood loss is that when you give the patient fluid,

55
00:03:47,700 --> 00:03:51,220
or when the patient eventually gets fluid back into their system,

56
00:03:51,480 --> 00:03:54,040
that's going to dilute down the remaining red blood cells,

57
00:03:54,460 --> 00:03:58,340
and then that will result in a decrease in the hemoglobin and hematocrit.

58
00:03:58,340 --> 00:04:02,460
And so, again, another reason why these are just surrogates.

59
00:04:02,760 --> 00:04:06,040
But for our discussions and for the purposes of what we want to consider,

60
00:04:06,520 --> 00:04:10,440
I think that the hemoglobin and hematocrit and the red blood cell count

61
00:04:10,440 --> 00:04:15,960
are relatively useful for determining red blood cell mass.

62
00:04:16,920 --> 00:04:21,780
The technical definition of anemia is a hemoglobin less than 3.5 grams in males

63
00:04:21,780 --> 00:04:27,440
and a hemoglobin or a hemoglobin less than 12.5 grams in females.

64
00:04:28,100 --> 00:04:34,380
We accept a lower hemoglobin in females because females normally lose a little bit of blood

65
00:04:34,380 --> 00:04:43,320
during their menstrual cycle, and therefore their hemoglobin may be slightly lower in the normal female.

66
00:04:44,320 --> 00:04:48,500
When you think about anemias, there's a lot of ways to divide anemias.

67
00:04:48,500 --> 00:04:54,400
And I think this, for me, at least as a student, had made anemia a particularly difficult subject.

68
00:04:54,820 --> 00:04:58,280
For example, you could look at some textbooks, and some textbooks will say,

69
00:04:58,620 --> 00:05:02,700
divide anemias based on underproduction or overdestruction.

70
00:05:03,420 --> 00:05:06,120
And that would be one way to look at anemias.

71
00:05:06,540 --> 00:05:09,000
Another textbook might look at anemias and say,

72
00:05:09,400 --> 00:05:13,140
well, let's divide anemias based on problems with the red blood cell

73
00:05:13,140 --> 00:05:15,500
and problems with something other than the red blood cell,

74
00:05:15,500 --> 00:05:18,000
so extrinsic or intrinsic defects.

75
00:05:18,500 --> 00:05:20,580
That's another way to look at anemia.

76
00:05:21,100 --> 00:05:25,620
However, my personal feeling is that the best way for a student

77
00:05:25,620 --> 00:05:29,440
who's learning about anemia to understand anemia

78
00:05:29,440 --> 00:05:32,420
is to divide it based on the MCV.

79
00:05:32,760 --> 00:05:35,100
The MCV is the mean corpuscular volume,

80
00:05:35,500 --> 00:05:39,620
and it tells you basically, it gives you an estimate of the size of the red blood cell.

81
00:05:39,620 --> 00:05:46,140
And for me, if you understand anemia based on the size of the red blood cell,

82
00:05:46,500 --> 00:05:51,280
that makes it very easy to learn the different anemias.

83
00:05:51,420 --> 00:05:54,840
And you'll see why as I take you through the remainder of this lecture.

84
00:05:54,940 --> 00:06:00,200
But I'm fairly confident that once you learn anemias the way that I'm going to explain it to you,

85
00:06:00,460 --> 00:06:04,460
you'll actually be very comfortable with anemias, you'll be very comfortable with them clinically,

86
00:06:04,460 --> 00:06:07,420
you'll be very comfortable with them for examination purposes.

87
00:06:07,920 --> 00:06:12,400
And of course, anemias are something that we regularly and routinely see both clinically

88
00:06:12,400 --> 00:06:15,180
as well as on any type of examination.

89
00:06:16,020 --> 00:06:20,100
Let's just remind ourselves that the normal red blood cell has an MCV,

90
00:06:20,180 --> 00:06:23,540
or a mean corpuscular volume, of about 80 to 100.

91
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:24,800
That's the normal.

92
00:06:25,200 --> 00:06:26,540
80 to 100 would be normal.

93
00:06:26,800 --> 00:06:31,540
Less than 80 is considered microcytic, i.e. the cells are smaller than they normally should be,

94
00:06:31,540 --> 00:06:37,520
and greater than 100 is considered macrocytic, i.e. the cells are bigger than they normally should be.

95
00:06:37,900 --> 00:06:40,500
And so we're going to divide anemia into three types,

96
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:46,240
microcytic anemias, macrocytic anemias, and normocytic anemias.

97
00:06:46,660 --> 00:06:50,400
And so these are the next three sections that you're going to cover,

98
00:06:50,600 --> 00:06:53,920
and we'll do them, obviously, we'll take them one at a time and do them sequentially.

99
00:06:54,260 --> 00:06:55,300
So let's stop there.

100
00:06:55,380 --> 00:06:59,700
That's the basic introduction, and the next section is going to deal with microcytic anemias.

